abstract: >-
  Code, data and results for the paper. Offline retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) on edge devices is updated
  through signed knowledge packs that carry text and precomputed embedding vectors. A signature proves who
  published a pack, not that its vectors encode its text. The paper measures this text-vector integrity gap: a
  verifier that accepts vectors within a cosine tolerance concedes a share of queries given by the distribution
  of angular retrieval margins (the margin law); tolerance-based audits of compressed vector stores leave up to
  all queries exposed, as an executed attack on a FAISS product-quantisation index shows; and code-level
  verification with verifier-decided ambiguity and canonicalisation closes the gap. The repository contains the
  reference verifier, the experiment and analysis scripts, the SQuAD-derived data, all per-query results and the
  protocol. Code: MIT; SQuAD-derived data and results containing SQuAD text: CC BY-SA 4.0 (see
  DATA_LICENSES.md).
